    // Importance of the Notch sprite:
	/* The Notch editor sprite has several sections. We need to account for, and compensate for, overdraw rendering, and, top-down
	      rendering artifacts. This is a 32x32px image. The primary section of the square sprite is **24x28**, to fit in the hex grid offsets.
	      The notch shape is interlocking. Original 16x16px Ultima V source sprites are placed into the main sprite as a 4x4 config.
	      The origin 16x16 section of the 4x4 group maintains the tiling pattern despite the discarded pixels outside the notch.
	    Overlap section: The top two px rows/lines are hard overlap space. We use this space for sprite features that are tall like mountains
	      and trees. The next two lines are secondary overlap - we might use these for sprite features that extend upward. Overlay rows will
	      overdraw the tile above. The fourth px row of the top overlap section is used for detail overlapping, _but is prone to artifacting_.
	    Notch sections: Red colored middle top-left and top-right sections. We cut the black pixels (background color) from the notch area,
	      leaving the detailed pixels. This cleans up messy offset overlapping to look as close to aligned as possible.
	    Sprite body: As noted, the 16x16px source sprite is placed into the 32x32px area as 4 conjoined source sprites. We then shape the sprite
	      with the punchout shape seen.
	    Bottom overlap: The last row is normally cut. Corner case artifacts like Surf use the Bottom row for overlap, vs the usual Top row overlap,
	      otherwise Surf causes artifact pixels inbetween decors in adjacent lands above Surf.

	    And that's how we accurately use square tiling on a hex grid! :D

	    The one gotcha is that the X and Y axis spacing is offset/squished by ~1/4px, which you can see when zoomed in,
	      so tiny corners of the sprites overlap. Solution (perfection) is to offest this with _much_ higher res sprites.
	*/


